<GuideArticleLayout
  title="Packing with boxes you already have"
  description="How to treat carton stock as a real constraint, what available quantity changes, and when a hybrid packing workflow becomes useful."
  category="Existing inventory"
  readTime="5 min read"
>
  <h2>Your inventory is part of the packing problem</h2>

  <p>
    When you already own cartons, the question is no longer
    just “what box size would work?” It becomes “what can I
    pack with the box types and quantities I actually have?”
  </p>

  <p>
    In Packmetry’s existing-box workflow, available quantity
    is a real limit. If a carton type has one unit available,
    a plan cannot silently open a second carton of that type.
    A zero quantity means that carton cannot be used.
  </p>

  <div class="pm-guide-note">
    <strong>Inventory means inventory</strong>
    <p>
      The existing-box workflow does not invent purchase
      cartons when your stock runs out. It works only with
      the carton types and quantities you entered.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use multiple carton types when that reflects reality</h2>

  <p>
    Real stockrooms are rarely made of one perfect box size.
    Entering several carton types gives the solver a more
    realistic set of choices. A verified plan may use one
    type exclusively or combine different types when that is
    a better fit for the order.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is also why carton quantity should be maintained
    carefully. A mathematically suitable carton is not a
    usable option if there is no stock available.
  </p>

  <h2>Hybrid is different from “have boxes”</h2>

  <p>
    Hybrid packing is useful when existing stock should be
    used first but buying additional cartons is acceptable
    for the remainder. It answers a different operational
    question from strict inventory-only packing.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practical terms, the three workflows are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Need boxes:</strong> find suitable cartons for
      the items you need to pack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Have boxes:</strong> pack only into the stock
      and quantities you entered.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hybrid:</strong> use existing stock and cover
      the remainder when inventory is insufficient.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Review inventory usage, not only the 3D picture</h2>

  <p>
    A successful plan should tell you both how the items were
    arranged and what happened to your carton stock. The
    inventory summary is useful because it makes the
    operational consequence of the plan visible: what was
    used and what remains.
  </p>

  <p>
    The 3D arrangement explains fit. Inventory usage explains
    the stock decision. Together they give a more complete
    view than either one on its own.
  </p>
</GuideArticleLayout>
